{% extends "@core/docs/_layout.twig" %}

{% block doc_content %}
    <h1>Pagination</h1>
    <p>
        Rhapsody includes a <code>Rhapsody\Core\Pagination</code> class that makes breaking large datasets into navigable pages straightforward, with optional support for custom CSS classes, a custom base URL, windowed page ranges with First/Last links, a jump-to-page menu, AJAX-friendly data attributes, and rendering through your own Twig template.
    </p>

    <h2>1. Model Setup</h2>
    <p>Your model needs two methods: one to count total records, and one to fetch a specific slice using <code>LIMIT</code> and <code>OFFSET</code>.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// app/Models/User.php
public function countAll(): int
{
    $stmt = $this->db->query("SELECT COUNT(user_id) FROM users");
    return (int) $stmt->fetchColumn();
}

public function findPaginated(int $limit, int $offset): array
{
    $sql = "SELECT ... FROM users ... LIMIT :limit OFFSET :offset";
    $stmt = $this->db->prepare($sql);
    $stmt->bindParam(':limit', $limit, PDO::PARAM_INT);
    $stmt->bindParam(':offset', $offset, PDO::PARAM_INT);
    $stmt->execute();
    return $stmt->fetchAll();
}</code></pre>

    <h2>2. Controller Logic</h2>
    <p>Instantiate the <code>Pagination</code> class with the total number of items, the desired limit, and the current page.</p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// app/Controllers/PageController.php
use Rhapsody\Core\Pagination;
use Rhapsody\Core\Request;

public function showUsers(Request $request): Response
{
    $usersPerPage = (int) $request->getQueryParam('limit', 10);
    $currentPage  = (int) $request->getQueryParam('page', 1);

    $totalUsers = $this->userModel->countAll();

    $pagination = new Pagination($totalUsers, $usersPerPage, $currentPage);

    // Optional: add custom CSS classes to the rendered navigation
    $pagination->setCssClasses([
        'container' => 'pagination-container',
        'list'      => 'pagination-list',
        'item'      => 'pagination-item',
        'active'    => 'is-active',
        'disabled'  => 'is-disabled',
        'link'      => 'pagination-link',
    ]);

    $users = $this->userModel->findPaginated(
        $pagination->getLimit(),
        $pagination->getOffset()
    );

    return $this->view('home/users.twig', [
        'users'      => $users,
        'pagination' => $pagination,
    ]);
}</code></pre>

    <h2>3. Rendering in the View</h2>
    <p>
        In your Twig template, call the <code>render()</code> method on the pagination object. It takes no arguments
        and returns a ready-to-use HTML string:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-twig">&lt;!-- views/home/users.twig --&gt;
&lt;div class="flex justify-center mt-8"&gt;
    &#123;&#123; pagination.render()|raw &#125;&#125;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
        The rendered markup is a <code>&lt;nav&gt;</code> containing a Previous link, a link for every page number,
        and a Next link — with the current page shown as inactive text rather than a link, and Previous/Next shown
        as disabled text at the start/end of the range. If there's only one page total, <code>render()</code> returns
        an empty string.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Customizing Appearance</h2>
    <p>
        <code>setCssClasses()</code> accepts any subset of the following keys — anything you don't override keeps its
        default value, which matches the framework's original unstyled markup exactly, so calling code that never
        touches this method sees no visual change:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>container</code> – wraps the whole nav in a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with this class (only added if you set a non-empty value)</li>
        <li><code>list</code> – class on the <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code></li>
        <li><code>item</code> – class on each <code>&lt;li&gt;</code></li>
        <li><code>link</code> – class on each clickable page link</li>
        <li><code>disabled</code> – class on the disabled Previous/Next text</li>
        <li><code>active</code> – class on the current page's text</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Custom Base URL</h2>
    <p>
        By default, page links are relative (<code>?page=2</code>), resolving against whatever URL the current page
        was requested on. If you're rendering pagination somewhere that URL isn't the right target — for example, a
        listing reachable from more than one route — use <code>setBaseUrl()</code> to make links absolute to a
        specific path instead:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$pagination->setBaseUrl('/users/list');
// Links become "/users/list?page=2" instead of "?page=2"</code></pre>

    <h2>6. Preserving Query Parameters</h2>
    <p>
        Any query parameters already present on the current request (other than <code>page</code>) are carried
        forward onto every page link automatically — for example, if the page was requested with
        <code>?limit=25&amp;sort=name</code>, every rendered link will include <code>&amp;limit=25&amp;sort=name</code>
        as well. This happens automatically by reading the current request's query string; there's no method to add
        parameters that aren't already part of it.
    </p>

        <h2>7. Windowed Pages, First & Last Links</h2>
    <p>
        By default, <code>render()</code> shows every page number — unchanged from the original behavior. For large
        page counts, pass <code>maxLinks</code> (either via <code>setMaxLinks()</code> as a standing default, or as a
        one-off <code>render()</code> option) to show a window of pages centered on the current one, with
        <strong>First</strong>/<strong>Last</strong> links and an ellipsis appearing automatically whenever page 1 or
        the last page falls outside that window:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">// Standing default — every render() call after this uses a 7-page window
$pagination->setMaxLinks(7);

// One-off override for a single render() call
echo $pagination->render(['maxLinks' => 7]);
// e.g. with 20 total pages, on page 10:
// Previous  First … 8 9 [10] 11 12 … Last  Next</code></pre>

    <h2>8. Jump-to-Page Menu</h2>
    <p>
        Enable a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> that lets visitors jump straight to any page, rendered immediately
        after the nav:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$pagination->setShowJumpMenu(true);
echo $pagination->render();</code></pre>
    <p>
        It works with no JavaScript — a <strong>Go</strong> button submits a standard GET form that preserves every
        other current query parameter as hidden inputs — and progressively enhances to jump immediately on
        <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> change when JavaScript is available. To place the menu somewhere other than
        right after the nav, call <code>renderJumpMenu()</code> on its own instead of passing <code>showJumpMenu</code>
        to <code>render()</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-twig">&#123;&#123; pagination.render()|raw &#125;&#125;
&lt;div class="mt-4"&gt;
    &#123;&#123; pagination.renderJumpMenu()|raw &#125;&#125;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

    <h2>9. AJAX Data Attributes</h2>
    <p>
        <code>getDataAttributes()</code> returns an associative array of <code>data-*</code> attributes describing
        the current pagination state, for a JavaScript-driven pagination widget to read:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$pagination->getDataAttributes();
// [
//     'data-pagination'   => '',
//     'data-current-page' => 3,
//     'data-total-pages'  => 12,
//     'data-per-page'     => 10,
//     'data-total-items'  => 117,
// ]</code></pre>

    <h2>10. Rendering a Custom Twig Template</h2>
    <p>
        Instead of the built-in markup, hand off rendering to your own Twig template with
        <code>renderTemplate()</code>. The <code>Pagination</code> instance is passed into the template as
        <code>pagination</code>, giving you direct access to <code>pagination.pageRange</code>,
        <code>pagination.previousPage</code>, <code>pagination.nextPage</code>, and <code>pagination.url(n)</code> —
        set <code>setMaxLinks()</code> beforehand if you want <code>pagination.pageRange</code> to reflect a window
        rather than every page:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-php">$pagination->setMaxLinks(5);
echo $pagination->renderTemplate('@app/components/pagination.twig');</code></pre>
    <pre><code class="language-twig">{# views/components/pagination.twig #}
&lt;nav&gt;
    &#123;&#123;# pagination.previousPage %} &lt;a href="&#123;&#123; pagination.url(pagination.previousPage) &#125;&#125;"&gt;&larr;&lt;/a&gt; &#123;&#123;% endif %&#125;

    &#123;&#123;% for page in pagination.pageRange %&#125;
        &lt;a href="&#123;&#123; pagination.url(page) &#125;&#125;"&gt;&#123;&#123; page &#125;&#125;&lt;/a&gt;
    &#123;&#123;% endfor %&#125;

    &#123;&#123;# pagination.nextPage %} &lt;a href="&#123;&#123; pagination.url(pagination.nextPage) &#125;&#125;"&gt;&rarr;&lt;/a&gt; &#123;&#123;% endif %&#125;
&lt;/nav&gt;</code></pre>
    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Note:</strong> <code>renderTemplate()</code> resolves the framework's Twig environment via
        <code>Container::getInstance()</code>, so it requires a bootstrapped container — see the
        <a href="{{ base_url }}/docs/container">Service Container</a> documentation. Plain <code>render()</code> has
        no such requirement and works anywhere, including isolated unit tests.
    </div>

    <h2>11. New CSS Class Keys</h2>
    <p>In addition to the keys from section 4, <code>setCssClasses()</code> now also accepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><code>ellipsis</code> – the "&hellip;" shown between a First/Last link and the windowed page range</li>
        <li><code>jumpForm</code> – the jump-to-page <code>&lt;form&gt;</code></li>
        <li><code>jumpLabel</code> – the "Jump to page:" <code>&lt;label&gt;</code></li>
        <li><code>jumpMenu</code> – the <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> itself</li>
        <li><code>jumpButton</code> – the "Go" fallback <code>&lt;button&gt;</code></li>
    </ul>

    <h2>12. AJAX Pagination (Zero Full-Page Reloads)</h2>
    <p>
        Combine <code>toAjaxPayload()</code> with <code>Request::wantsJson()</code> to get near-instant page transitions on a rapidly changing dataset, without giving up a fast, fully-rendered initial page load or risking stale cached HTML — the same controller and the same route serve both the normal page load and the AJAX request, so there's no separate API endpoint or duplicated list-rendering logic to maintain.
    </p>

    <h3>A. Include the script once</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-twig">{{ '{%' }} include "@core/partials/pagination-ajax.twig" {{ '%}' }}</code></pre>

    <h3>B. Wire up the controller</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php">public function index(Request $request): Response
{
    $pagination = new Pagination($totalItems, 20, (int) $request->get('page', 1));
    $products   = $this->productModel->paginated($pagination->getLimit(), $pagination->getOffset());

    $listHtml = $this->twig->render('@app/partials/product-list.twig', ['products' => $products]);

    if ($request->wantsJson()) {
        return $this->json($pagination->toAjaxPayload($listHtml));
    }

    return $this->view('@app/marketplace/index.twig', [
        'products'   => $products,
        'listHtml'   => $listHtml,
        'pagination' => $pagination,
    ]);
}</code></pre>

    <h3>C. Wrap the markup</h3>
    <p>
        <code>data-list-target</code>, <code>data-pagination-target</code>, and <code>data-jump-target</code> tell
        the script which elements to swap — the last is optional, only needed when
        <code>setShowJumpMenu(true)</code> is in use:
    </p>
    <pre><code class="language-twig">&lt;div data-ajax-pagination
     data-list-target="#product-list"
     data-pagination-target="#product-pagination"
     data-jump-target="#product-jump"&gt;

    &lt;div id="product-list"&gt;
        &#123;&#123; listHtml|raw &#125;&#125;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div id="product-pagination"&gt;
        &#123;&#123; pagination.render(&#123;showJumpMenu: false&#125;)|raw &#125;&#125;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div id="product-jump"&gt;
        &#123;&#123; pagination.renderJumpMenu()|raw &#125;&#125;
    &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

    <div class="bg-blue-100 border-l-4 border-blue-500 text-blue-700 p-4">
        <strong>Progressive by design:</strong> every link in <code>render()</code>'s output is a real,
        fully-working <code>href</code>, and the jump-menu is a real <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> — with JavaScript
        disabled (or before the script has finished loading) the page just navigates normally. The script only
        intercepts what it can enhance.
    </div>
{% endblock %}
